> 
>   I think you're on to something there.  Conducts can make 
> Nethack nearly impossible.  If that's not enough, then players 
> aren't really interested in a more difficult Nethack.

The problem with conducts, for me, is that they don't affect the 
difficulty in the right places.

I don't think I've ever lost a character between the castle and the 
sanctum.  By the time I enter Gehennom, there's nothing in there that 
can scare me.  Hell, the only reason demon lords are annoying is because 
they won't stand still on one level and let me finish killing them.   
(Until I stand on the up-stairs and cut off their escape.)

I lose a lot of characters in the early game, however.  The mines can be 
a killer, and quest nemeses often make me nervous.

Conducts generally make the early game harder, but don't do much to the 
midgame.  If I can survive to Gehennom with a conduct intact, I'm not 
going to have any problems surviving to the sanctum with it.  (Never 
used a wish and never polymorphed an item are the two main exceptions to 
this--but they don't make much of a difference.)


>   So it doesn't seem so much that players think Nethack is too 
> easy.  It's more that they don't think Nethack is interesting 
> enough.

Yes.


>   Gehennom gets mentioned a lot in this context.  Players think
> its too long and boring.  They want a more interesting challenge
> instead.
> 
>   Yet I now realize that's why Gehennom is the way it is.  No one
> gets a free ride through it on the Ascension Run.  Every 
> ascension, first time or thirtieth time, has to slog up through 
> Gehennom.  There are ways to alleviate the pain, but no way to 
> avoid it completely.

Going *up* through Gehennom isn't the problem.  You know where the 
stairs are.  I actually rather enjoy the run up.

The problem is the way down.  Almost every level in Gehennom is exactly 
the same--featureless mazes.  There's none of the variety found in the 
main dungeon.  No beehives, no throne rooms, no treasure zoos, no 
stores, no vaults, no altar rooms, just more mazes with the occasional 
special level.

And each one of those mazes has to be meticulously explored to find the 
down staircase.  Since mazes restrict visibility far more than the rooms 
in the main dungeon, this takes a lot more walking around and mapping.

To make things even worse, mazes have far less tactical interest than 
the main dungeon.  Since they consist entirely of one-tile-wide 
corridors, it's trivial to stand in a bottleneck where at most two 
enemies can attack.  There's nothing to compare with the challenge of 
surviving an attack by a trio of ants in the first levels of the game.


>   To a (mere ;^) mortal Gehennom is endlessly terrifying, but not
> boring.  To the regular ascender, it's endlessly boring.  So much
> so that some players will start a new game rather than go through
> it again.

I started finding Gehennom boring after my first ascension.  Actually, I 
think I started finding it boring *during* my first ascension.

In the main dungeon, Nethack is an endless series of discoveries.  
You're constantly entering new rooms, and each new room is slightly 
different.  Every other level has some interesting feature on it--a 
shop, a vault, a swarm of orcs.

This stops dead once you pass the castle.


>   And that's the way it should be.  Ascension should not be the
> natural outcome of most Nethack games.  An ascension should also 
> mean more than that the player has skill in Nethack.  It should 
> mean the player spent a lot of time/effort in that game.

Nethack is not EverQuest.  The barrier to winning should not be a 
willingness to endure tedium!

